<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wilhelmshöhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe is a large landscape park on a hillside west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, laid out by the landgraves of Hesse-Kassel from the late 17th century. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules monument</a>, water cascades and fountains, and Wilhelmshöhe Palace, built from 1786 for Landgrave William IX. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was held at the palace after his capture at Sedan in 1870, and the park became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2013.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe.jpg" alt="Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe" width="318" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hendrik Thole · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herkules_Monument,_Bergpark_Wilhelmsh%C3%B6he,_Kassel_2004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
